<h2>Evaluating Different Sectional Configurations</h2>
<p>Dividing a model into smaller sections can make it easier to pack and handle, but each connection introduces a joint that needs to work within the finished display.</p>
<p>For Aviation Garage, we evaluated multiple sectional configurations for both the building and its base. The aim was to identify where separation would support transport and handling while preserving the appearance and integrity of the assembled model.</p>
<p>The building and base were considered separately. A configuration that worked for the base did not necessarily offer the same benefits for the architecture above it.</p>

<h2>A Two-Part Base and a One-Piece Building</h2>
<p>Following the tests, we chose to split the base into two sections and retain the building structure as one piece.</p>
<p>The divided base provided flexibility for packing and handling. Keeping the building intact avoided introducing joints through the architecture and preserved a continuous presentation of its form and details.</p>
<p>This selective approach allowed us to address transport requirements without dividing every component of the model.</p>
